\description{
Summarize, in each grafted phylogeny, the species of each clade listed in \code{total_richness_per_clades}:
species of the backbone, grafted species, and unsampled species (species of the clade that are neither in the backbone nor grafted).
It then derives the sampling fraction of each clade that can be used in downstream analyses
to account for the clade-specific sampling fractions (e.g., diversification models).

The function works a posteriori, on the output of \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa()}} or \code{\link[=graft_missing_clades]{phylograftR::graft_missing_clades()}}
(a phylogeny, a set of simulations, or the list of maps obtained with a \code{"multiSimmap"}).
The counts are taken in each grafted phylogeny. With \code{graft_missing_taxa()}, they can differ among simulations and maps, as a taxon can end up in different clades.
With \code{graft_missing_clades()}, they are the same in all the phylogenies, as each missing clade is always grafted on the stem of its sister clade.
}
\details{
\subsection{Species of a clade}{

The species of a clade are the tips that descend from its MRCA in the grafted phylogeny: the backbone species of the clade, and the grafted taxa that were grafted within it.
It depends on the phylogeny, as a taxon grafted in a target clade can fall in a clade nested within it, and as the taxa grafted on the stem branch of a clade
are sister to the clade, so they are not part of it. The exception is the clade of a single tip of \verb{$Target_clade}: its terminal branch is its only branch,
so the taxa grafted on it are part of the clade. For \verb{$Sister_clade}, the missing clades are grafted on the stem branch of the sister clade and are never part of it.
}

\subsection{Unsampled species and sampling fraction}{

For a clade with a total richness provided, \code{nb_unsampled_species = total_richness - nb_backbone_species - nb_grafted_species}.
For a clade without a total richness (\code{NA}), the unsampled species are those identified in the largest clades nested within it that have a total richness (none if there is none),
and its total richness is derived as \code{nb_backbone_species + nb_grafted_species + nb_unsampled_species} (it can differ among phylogenies).
The sampling fraction is \code{(nb_backbone_species + nb_grafted_species) / total_richness}.

In problematic cases, the number of species of a clade can be higher than its total richness: for example, a taxon known to belong to a genus can be grafted in a subclade
of this genus whose provided total richness did not include it. The number of unsampled species is then negative and the sampling fraction is higher than 1.
These values are returned as they are, with a warning listing the clades and the number of phylogenies concerned, so the clades and the total richness provided can be reviewed.
When a clade without a total richness is derived from nested clades, the negative values of the nested clades are counted as \code{0}.
}
